<section class="money-example" data-money-area="home" aria-labelledby="offset-picture-title">
  <p class="money-example-kicker">An offset, in three steps</p>
  <h2 id="offset-picture-title">The debt stays. The interest bill shrinks.</h2>
  <div class="offset-picture">
    <div><svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8 30 32 10l24 20M15 26v30h34V26M27 56V38h10v18"/></svg><span>1 · Your home loan</span><strong>$600,000</strong><span>This is what you owe.</span></div>
    <div><svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="8" y="20" width="48" height="35" rx="8"/><path d="M18 20V10h28v10M26 30h12M32 36v10M26 41h12"/></svg><span>2 · Money in a full offset</span><strong>$40,000</strong><span>Your cash stays in a separate account.</span></div>
    <div><svg viewBox="0 0 64 64" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="32" cy="32" r="24"/><path d="M20 32h24M32 20v24"/><path d="m47 8 9 9M8 47l9 9"/></svg><span>3 · Balance charged interest</span><strong>$560,000</strong><span>$600,000 − $40,000. Your debt is still $600,000.</span></div>
  </div>
  <p class="money-example-assumptions">Illustration of a 100% offset. Your repayments, access rules, fees and offset percentage depend on the loan. Extra repayments reduce the actual debt; offset cash does not.</p>
